é¨Ös¸c ŽÖÙ7Lº¶ýÔ‘ý>qk°õö…{"url":"/gcsl/language/functions-and-effects/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/functions-and-effects/","content":"Functions and Effects. A function's signature says what it accepts, what it returns, and whether calling it can perform effects. This makes executable configuration reviewable: a pure calculation cannot silently run a process or read a file. Choosing a function form # Use fn for a private function and def for a function exposed as a field. Both forms declare parameter and result types. A function expression can also be assigned to a variable or passed to another function. The marker before the result type selects the effect: MarkerMeaning : Pure computation; no effects or awaiting. -> Impure computation; effects and awaiting are allowed. ->> Impure computation memoized by its arguments. This private pure function accepts a named argument and a default: Copyfn scale(value: Int, factor: Int = 2): Int { value * factor } scale(value = 21) Positional and named arguments are evaluated in source order. A trailing block supplies the last parameter, which is useful for functions that accept a configuration record. A default can be any expression. It can use the parameters before it and anything visible where the function is written: Copylet suffix = \".o\" fn object(source: String, output: String = \"{source}{suffix}\"): String { output } main = object(\"main.c\") lib = object(\"lib.c\", output = \"lib.a\") A default is evaluated each time a call leaves its parameter out, after the arguments that are written and before the function's body runs. It has the function's effect, so a pure function's default cannot call an impure function. A default cannot use its own parameter or a later one. Only a call by the function's own name uses its defaults. A call through a copy, such as g after let g = object, or through a parameter must supply every argument. After a named argument, a name on its own is shorthand for passing the variable of that name to the parameter of that name. These two calls are the same: Copyfn build(source: String, output: String, compiler: String): String { \"{compiler} {source} -o {output}\" } let compiler = \"cc\" short = build(\"main.c\", output = \"main.o\", compiler) long = build(\"main.c\", output = \"main.o\", compiler = compiler) Before any named argument, a name on its own is an ordinary positional argument. Generic functions # A function can work on values of any type. Name its type parameters in angle brackets after its name, and use them as types in its signature and body: Copyfn first<T>(items: List<T>, fallback: T): T { if len(items) > 0 { items[0] } else { fallback } } port = first([8080, 9090], 80) name = first([], \"none\") Each call works out the type parameters from its arguments: here T is Int in the first call and String in the second. Inside the function, a value of type T can be passed on, returned, or put in a list or record, but nothing else, because T could be anything. A call where nothing binds a type parameter, such as one that only returns a List<T>, is an error. Methods # A method is a function you call on a value with a dot. Write its receiver in parentheses before its name: Copyfn (n: Int) twice(): Int { n * 2 } fn (items: List<T>) first_or<T>(fallback: T): T { if len(items) > 0 { items[0] } else { fallback } } doubled = 21.twice() port = [8080].first_or(80) The value before the dot is the receiver. GCSL chooses the method by the receiver's type, so methods with the same name can serve different types. When more than one fits, the one for the more specific type is used: a method for List<Int> is chosen over one for List<T> when the receiver is a List<Int>. A method's other parameters, defaults and effects work as a function's do. A method has no name of its own: twice alone is not a variable, and x.twice without parentheses reads a field. A record field and a method with the same name are an error when both could apply. Methods are declared at the top level of a file. A fn method is visible only in its own file. A def method is also visible to the other files of its directory and the directories below it, and to every file that imports its module, whatever the form of the import. In the REPL, methods are visible to later lines. Calling for effects # A call to an impure or memoized function can stand alone as a statement, outside a pure function, to run it for its effects. Its value is discarded, whatever it is, and anything it defers is awaited first, so no effect is lost: Copyfn build(source: String) -> String { sh(\"make -C {source}\") sh(\"make -C {source} install\") = source } Any other value standing alone is an error, because discarding it would do nothing: a pure expression has no effects to keep. Make it the block's result with = ..., bind it with let, or remove it. Captures and recursion # Closures hold copies of the values they capture. Rebinding a variable later doesn't change an existing closure. An inner function cannot rebind an outer function's variable. Top-level named functions are available regardless of textual order and can refer to each other within the file. Workspace files still need an acyclic dependency order: functions in two files cannot make those files need each other. Put mutually dependent definitions together or extract their shared dependencies. Memoization # A memoized function reuses a successful result for the same arguments in the same execution context. The cache belongs to the closure, so two closures created from one function expression have separate entries. Memoization also accounts for supported ambient inputs such as a caller path when the function reads it. This is not a persistent build cache. External changes to files or a command's environment do not invalidate a stored result. Use ->> only when reusing the first result within that run is correct. Cached keys and results consume memory for the lifetime of their execution context. Read Deferred Work before using futures in function signatures; a bare type's present-or-future meaning depends on its position and the function's effect.","word_count":1022,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Functions and Effects"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"choosing-a-function-form","text":"Choosing a function form #","location":37},{"element":"h2","id":"generic-functions","text":"Generic functions #","location":369},{"element":"h2","id":"methods","text":"Methods #","location":499},{"element":"h2","id":"calling-for-effects","text":"Calling for effects #","location":724},{"element":"h2","id":"captures-and-recursion","text":"Captures and recursion #","location":824},{"element":"h2","id":"memoization","text":"Memoization #","location":901}]}},"word_data":{"closur":[{"position":828,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":844,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":924,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":927,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"not":[{"position":636,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":955,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":969,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"listt":[{"position":403,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":495,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":534,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":606,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"doesn":[{"position":840,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"their":[{"position":898,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":997,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"copi":[{"position":274,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":830,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"level":[{"position":669,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":854,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"anoth":[{"position":77,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"privat":[{"position":46,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":112,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"result":[{"position":62,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position"